+# CLI Contract: `python -m bookmarks`
+
+**Feature**: `001-bookmark-manager-cli` | **Date**: 2026-11-16
+
+This is the complete external interface of the tool — the command grammar, the byte-level output
+formats, and the exit-status map. It is the contract tests assert against. Nothing here is
+optional and nothing beyond here is offered: the five commands are the whole scope.
+
+---
+
+## Invocation
+
+```text
+python -m bookmarks [--db PATH] COMMAND [ARGS...]
+```
+
+`src/` must be on `PYTHONPATH` (FR-026). `--db` is a global option and must appear **before** the
+command, as the grammar above shows.
+
+| Option       | Default                            | Meaning |
+| ------------ | ---------------------------------- | ------- |
+| `--db PATH`  | `bookmarks.db` in the current directory | The SQLite store file (FR-002) |
+
+Because the default is relative to the working directory, running from two different directories
+uses two different collections.
+
+`--help` on the tool or on any command prints usage to stdout and exits **0**.
+
+---
+
+## Commands
+
+### `add URL [--title TITLE] [--tags TAGS]`
+
+Stores a bookmark and prints its identifier.
+
+| Argument      | Required | Default | Meaning |
+| ------------- | -------- | ------- | ------- |
+| `URL`         | yes      | —       | Must begin with the literal `http://` or `https://` (FR-007) |
+| `--title`     | no       | `""`    | Display title (FR-008) |
+| `--tags`      | no       | none    | Comma-separated tag list, e.g. `python,cli` (FR-009) |
+
+**stdout**: the new identifier followed by a newline, and nothing else (FR-005).
+
+```console
+$ python -m bookmarks add https://docs.python.org --title "Python docs" --tags python,docs
+1
+```
+
+Identifiers start at 1 in a fresh store, increase, and are never reissued — not even after the
+bookmark holding one is deleted (FR-006).
+
+Tags are trimmed, lower-cased, de-duplicated, and empty entries dropped before storage
+(FR-010); see [data-model.md](../data-model.md) for the worked cases.
+
+**Failure**: a URL failing the prefix check writes a diagnostic to stderr naming the rejected URL
+and the accepted schemes, stores nothing, writes nothing to stdout, and exits **2** (FR-007).
+
+### `list [--tag TAG]`
+
+Prints bookmarks in ascending identifier order (FR-011).
+
+| Argument  | Required | Meaning |
+| --------- | -------- | ------- |
+| `--tag`   | no       | Keep only bookmarks carrying this tag (FR-012) |
+
+`TAG` is trimmed and lower-cased before comparison, and matched against **whole tags only**: so
+`--tag Python`, `--tag python` and `--tag " PYTHON "` select the same bookmarks, while `--tag py`
+does **not** match a bookmark tagged `python`.
+
+**stdout**: the TSV record format below, one bookmark per line. No match prints nothing and
+exits 0 (FR-015).
+
+### `search TEXT`
+
+Prints every bookmark whose title **or** URL contains `TEXT`, disregarding capitalisation, in
+ascending identifier order (FR-013).
+
+| Argument | Required | Meaning |
+| -------- | -------- | ------- |
+| `TEXT`   | yes      | Substring to look for; matching ignores case |
+
+Empty `TEXT` matches every bookmark, since every string contains the empty string. `TEXT` is
+matched literally — it holds no wildcards, so `100%` searches for `100%`.
+
+**stdout**: the TSV record format below. No match prints nothing and exits 0 (FR-015).
+
+### `delete ID`
+
+Removes the bookmark with that identifier, together with its tags, so it appears in no later
+listing, search or export (FR-016).
+
+| Argument | Required | Meaning |
+| -------- | -------- | ------- |
+| `ID`     | yes      | Identifier of the bookmark to remove; must be an integer |
+
+**stdout**: nothing. **Exit**: 0 on success.
+
+**Failures**, which must never be confused:
+
+| Case                          | Exit | Reason |
+| ----------------------------- | ---- | ------ |
+| `ID` is not an integer        | 2    | Usage mistake (FR-024) |
+| No bookmark holds `ID`        | 3    | Well-formed but absent; store left unchanged (FR-017) |
+
+### `export --format {csv,json}`
+
+Prints the whole collection in ascending identifier order.
+
+| Argument    | Required | Values         |
+| ----------- | -------- | -------------- |
+| `--format`  | yes      | `csv`, `json`  |
+
+Any other value is a usage mistake: diagnostic on stderr, exit **2** (FR-024). Formats are
+specified byte-for-byte below.
+
+---
+
+## Output formats
+
+### TSV record format — `list` and `search`
+
+Exactly four tab-separated fields per line, in this order (FR-014):
+
+```text
+id <TAB> url <TAB> title <TAB> tags
+```
+
+- `tags` is the bookmark's tags joined by `,` in alphabetical order; a bookmark with no tags has
+  an **empty** fourth field (the line still has three tabs).
+- An empty title is an empty field, not a placeholder.
+- Fields are emitted raw with **no escaping**. A title containing a tab or newline is stored and
+  returned unchanged, which makes this view ambiguous for such values — accepted deliberately,
+  because the lossless views are the CSV and JSON exports.
+
+```text
+1	https://docs.python.org	Python docs	docs,python
+2	https://example.com		
+```
+
+(Line 2 is a bookmark with no title and no tags: `2`, URL, empty, empty.)
+
+### CSV — `export --format csv`
+
+- First line is exactly the header `id,url,title,tags` (FR-018).
+- Then one row per bookmark in ascending identifier order, the `tags` column holding that
+  bookmark's tags joined by `,` in alphabetical order.
+- Fields containing a comma, a double quote or a line break are quoted, with embedded quotes
+  doubled, such that a standard CSV reader recovers the original values exactly (FR-019).
+- Every line, header included, ends with a single line feed `\n` — **not** `\r\n`. A line break
+  inside a quoted field is part of that field's value and is preserved as given (FR-019a).
+- An empty store prints the header line alone and exits 0 (FR-021).
+
+```csv
+id,url,title,tags
+1,https://docs.python.org,Python docs,"docs,python"
+2,https://example.com,"He said ""hi"", then
+left",
+```
+
+### JSON — `export --format json`
+
+- A single JSON array of objects in ascending identifier order, each with the keys `id`
+  (a number, not a string), `url`, `title` and `tags` (an array of strings in alphabetical
+  order) (FR-020).
+- Indented two spaces per nesting level, one key per line, ending with a single trailing
+  newline (FR-020a).
+- Characters outside ASCII are written literally as UTF-8, never as `\uXXXX` escapes (FR-020a).
+- An empty store prints `[]` followed by a newline and exits 0 (FR-021, FR-020a).
+
+```json
+[
+  {
+    "id": 1,
+    "url": "https://docs.python.org",
+    "title": "Python docs",
+    "tags": [
+      "docs",
+      "python"
+    ]
+  },
+  {
+    "id": 2,
+    "url": "https://example.com",
+    "title": "",
+    "tags": []
+  }
+]
+```
+
+---
+
+## Streams
+
+- All command **results** go to stdout; all **diagnostics** go to stderr (FR-023).
+- A failing command writes **nothing** to stdout — no partial results (FR-023).
+- Both streams are encoded UTF-8 regardless of the machine's locale, so a title, tag or URL
+  containing non-ASCII characters is emitted correctly instead of raising an encoding error
+  (FR-023a).
+- Output is plain text: no colour, no paging, no interactive prompting.
+
+---
+
+## Exit statuses
+
+This map is complete and exhaustive (FR-024).
+
+| Status | Meaning | Cases |
+| ------ | ------- | ----- |
+| **0** | Success | Any command that completed its work, including one that matched nothing (FR-015, FR-021, FR-022); also `--help` |
+| **1** | The store could not be used | Parent directory of `--db` does not exist; the path is an existing file that is not a bookmark database (plain text, an image, another program's database); the file system denies the required read or write access. Diagnostic names the path and the reason. No file or directory is created, nothing is written to stdout, and any existing file is left byte-for-byte unchanged — never overwritten, truncated, re-initialised or repaired (FR-003a, SC-009) |
+| **2** | A mistake in what the user supplied | A URL not beginning with `http://` or `https://` (FR-007); an unknown command; a missing required argument; a non-numeric `delete` identifier; an unsupported `--format`. Every case writes a diagnostic to stderr (FR-024) |
+| **3** | Deleting an identifier that does not exist | `delete ID` where `ID` is well-formed but no bookmark holds it. Reserved **exclusively** for this case (FR-017, FR-024) |
+
+Every diagnostic names what was invalid and what was expected; "error" or "invalid input" alone
+is insufficient (constitution, Principle II).
+
+---
+
+## Store behaviour
+
+| Situation | Behaviour |
+| --------- | --------- |
+| `--db` omitted | Store is `bookmarks.db` in the current working directory (FR-002) |
+| Store file absent, parent directory exists | Created with its schema on first use, then used (FR-003) |
+| Store file absent, parent directory missing | Exit 1; no directory is created — the tool never creates missing directories (FR-003a) |
+| Store exists from an earlier run | Opened and appended to, never reset or overwritten (FR-003) |
+| Store unrecognisable or inaccessible | Exit 1, file untouched (see status 1 above) |
+
+Bookmarks persist across separate invocations (FR-004). The tool serves one person on one
+machine; concurrent use of one store by several processes is out of scope.
+
+All commands require read **and** write access to the store, because any command may have to
+create it. A read-only store file therefore fails with status 1 even for `list` — a deliberate
+consequence of one uniform access requirement (research R-002).
